---

I was looking through the Claude Code source code when I found a function called [`matchesNegativeKeyword`](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/utils/userPromptKeywords.ts#L1-L11).

Claude Code is a coding agent capable of navigating repositories, editing files, running commands, managing permissions, and coordinating other agents. Somewhere inside all of that is a regular expression trying to answer a much older engineering question:

> Did the user just swear at us?

Naturally, I needed to know more.

My first interpretation was that I had discovered the F\*\*\* Index: F\*\*\*s per successful task. Lower is better. Probably.

The actual implementation is both less ridiculous and much more interesting, because it leads into a problem that every AI product eventually runs into: how do you know whether the AI is doing a good job?

## The F\*\*\* Index

The function is quite simple. It converts the user's prompt to lowercase and checks it against a list of negative words and phrases. This includes the usual abbreviations, several direct insults, and phrases such as "this sucks" and "so frustrating."

It then returns a Boolean.

No emotional analysis. No second model quietly judging the first model. Just `true` or `false`.

That value is passed into an analytics event called [`tengu_input_prompt`](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/utils/processUserInput/processTextPrompt.ts#L59-L64):

```ts
logEvent('tengu_input_prompt', {
	is_negative: isNegative,
	is_keep_going: isKeepGoing
});
```

For this particular event, the prompt itself is not included. Neither is the matching word, the number of matches, or some grand unified user happiness score. It is just a pair of Booleans attached to an event that can be enriched with information such as the session, model, client, and environment before entering a first-party analytics pipeline.

I could trace the event being created, sampled, enriched, and exported. I could not find the dashboard, query, or metric that eventually interprets it. So it would be misleading to call this Anthropic's official satisfaction score.

The detector is real. The F\*\*\* Index is mine.

## Sentiment Analysis, but Make It Regex

It is very easy to look at a regular expression inside one of the most advanced coding products in the world and find the contrast funny.

It is also probably a sensible engineering decision.

A regex is cheap, fast, deterministic, and easy to inspect. It adds no model latency, consumes no tokens, and cannot suddenly decide that the word "awful" feels positive today. If all you need is a rough signal across a large number of prompts, invoking another language model would be a remarkably expensive way to answer a yes-or-no question.

There is also a useful privacy distinction here. The internal prompt event receives the derived Boolean rather than the raw prompt. Claude Code has a separate, customer-configurable OpenTelemetry path for prompt logging, but [prompt content is redacted by default](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/utils/telemetry/events.ts#L13-L19). Raw prompt logging has to be explicitly enabled for that path.

So the simplicity is not really the problem.

The problem is deciding what the Boolean means.

A user might swear because Claude broke something. They might also swear because a test framework broke something, because an error message contains profanity, or because Claude produced something surprisingly good. Some people swear constantly. Others will remain exceptionally polite while watching an agent dismantle their application one file at a time.

Frustration is already difficult to detect. Even when you detect it correctly, it is not necessarily evidence of model failure.

The regex gives you a signal. It does not give you the story behind it.

## The Human-in-the-Loop Counter

The second Boolean in that event comes from [`matchesKeepGoingKeyword`](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/utils/userPromptKeywords.ts#L13-L27).

This function checks for three forms of continuation. A prompt containing only `continue` matches, while `keep going` and `go on` can appear inside a longer prompt. Curiously, `continue!` does not match because `continue` must be the entire trimmed prompt.

At first glance, this looks like an autonomy metric.

Every time the user types "continue," the autonomous coding agent has technically outsourced one more decision back to the human. Count enough of these and perhaps you have discovered how often Claude stops before the user believes the task is finished.

There is no evidence in the source that Anthropic actually calls it an autonomy metric. The client logs `is_keep_going`, but the interpretation happens somewhere beyond the code available here, if it happens at all.

And once again, the meaning is slippery.

A user might type "continue" because Claude stopped too early. Or because the task was deliberately split into stages. Or because the agent reached a permission boundary. Or because the user wanted to inspect the first half before allowing the second.

In those cases, asking for confirmation may be a feature rather than a failure.

An agent that never asks for input is not necessarily more autonomous. It may just be more willing to make assumptions.

## Success, According to a Loop

This ambiguity appears throughout the telemetry.

Claude Code records plenty of conventional operational information. API calls have success and error events. Retries have attempts and delays. Tool calls have duration, result size, and failure states. Sessions accumulate cost, token use, duration, and lines changed.

All of this is useful. None of it proves that the user got what they wanted.

The query loop itself has terminal reasons such as [`completed`, `model_error`, `aborted_tools`, and `max_turns`](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/query/transitions.ts#L8-L23). The SDK also returns a structured success result containing duration, turns, cost, usage, stop reason, and permission denials.

But [`isResultSuccessful`](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/utils/queryHelpers.ts#L48-L94) primarily decides success from the shape of the final message or an `end_turn` stop reason. In other words, success means the execution loop reached a valid ending.

It does not mean the architecture is good.

It does not mean the tests passed.

It does not even necessarily mean the user is happy.

This is the awkward difference between measuring normal software and measuring an AI agent. An API endpoint can be judged by whether it returned the expected response. An agent can return a perfectly valid response, modify several files, execute every command successfully, and still solve the wrong problem with great confidence.

## Just Ask the User?

Claude Code does also use a much more advanced satisfaction-detection system: occasionally, it asks.

The interface contains a feedback survey that says [`How is Claude doing this session?`](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/components/FeedbackSurvey/FeedbackSurveyView.tsx#L12-L21), with options for `Bad`, `Fine`, and `Good`. The response event includes the survey type and the latest assistant message ID.

This is much closer to direct evidence of satisfaction than a profanity detector. It is still not perfect.

Was the user rating the last answer, the entire session, the model, the permission prompts, or the fact that they had already spent twenty minutes fighting a dependency problem that Claude did not create?

Even explicit feedback requires interpretation.

Permission decisions provide another useful signal. Claude Code [records whether tools were automatically approved, temporarily approved, permanently approved, or rejected](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/hooks/toolPermission/permissionLogging.ts#L91-L235). It can also record how long the tool waited for the user and whether the user supplied corrective instructions.

Those instructions are not just analytics. [A rejection can become an errored tool result](https://github.com/codeaashu/claude-code/blob/main/src/services/tools/toolExecution.ts#L995-L1071) that the model reads before choosing another approach. Feedback supplied alongside an approval can also be appended after the successful result for the next step.

That tells us something interesting about autonomy. Human intervention is not always an interruption to be eliminated. Sometimes it is the mechanism that keeps the agent aligned with what the user actually wants.

## Measuring Vibes at Scale

The more of this telemetry I followed, the less convincing any single metric became.

Profanity can suggest frustration. Continuation prompts can suggest premature stopping. Permission rejections can suggest a bad action. Interruptions and rewinds can suggest that the agent went in the wrong direction. Errors, retries, and context limits can suggest technical difficulty.

They can all also mean something else.

The sensible approach is not to find one perfect measurement, because there probably is not one. It is to combine imperfect signals: explicit feedback, user behaviour, technical reliability, resource use, and task outcomes. Each captures a different part of the interaction, and each becomes dangerous when treated as the whole truth.

This is also where the distinction between logging and measuring becomes important. The source shows that Claude Code detects these signals and sends events. It does not show how those events are aggregated, what targets exist, or whether any of them influence product decisions.

Collecting a Boolean is easy.

Deciding what it means is the actual work.

## Closing Thoughts

The profanity detector is funny because it reduces a complicated human experience to a regular expression.

It is useful for exactly the same reason.

Production systems are full of simple heuristics sitting underneath sophisticated products. The good ones are not pretending to understand everything. They cheaply capture one small, observable fact that can be combined with other evidence later.

Claude Code cannot determine user satisfaction from whether somebody typed "WTF." It can, however, remember that it happened.

The difficult part is working out whether the model failed, the tooling failed, or the user was simply expressing heartfelt appreciation for a particularly good refactor.

For now, the F\*\*\* Index remains an imperfect metric.

But honestly, so are most of the others.
